<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Speyer</h1><p class="st-meta">1294 CE – 1797 CE · capital Speyer</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rhineland cathedral city that hosted imperial diets and the Imperial Chamber Court and gave Protestantism its name in 1529.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Speyer, the Roman Noviomagus, was the burial place of the Salian emperors, whose cathedral rose there from 1030. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> gradually wrested power from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, helped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-holy-roman-emperor">Henry V</a>'s charter of 1111, and in 1294 the bishop gave up most of his remaining rights, leaving the city self-governing under the emperor alone.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Patricians and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> shared power in the council. The city hosted some fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diets</a>; at the 1529 diet the Lutheran estates issued the 'protestation' that gave Protestants their name. From 1527 to 1689 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a>, the empire's highest court, sat in Speyer. The city turned Lutheran, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-speyer">Prince-Bishopric of Speyer</a> kept the cathedral Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1689 French troops burned Speyer during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, and the court moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar">Wetzlar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-first-republic">Revolutionary France</a> occupied the city in 1792 and annexed it in 1797. After 1816 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-palatinate">Bavarian Palatinate</a>, and it now lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1294 CE – 1797 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.45%;width:10.01%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1294 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Speyer</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Speyer, Spires</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1546&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-speyer" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Speyer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4874 458 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Speyer lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5194 714L5229 694M5194 714L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Speyer. <a href="/atlas?year=1546&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-speyer">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

